        {
          "text": "You found the two tiny steps: E to F and B to C. Those are [[semitone|semitones]]. Every other step is a [[tone|tone]]. Say the pattern like a chant: [[tone|tone]], [[tone|tone]], [[semitone|semitone]]. [[tone|Tone]], [[tone|tone]], [[tone|tone]], [[semitone|semitone]].",
          "exhibit": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "highlight": [
              "C4",
              "D4",
              "E4",
              "F4",
              "G4",
              "A4",
              "B4",
              "C5"
            ],
            "low": "C4",
            "high": "C5"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "The pattern makes the scale, not the letter C. Start on any note, follow the pattern, and you have a [[major-scale|major scale]]. C is special only because the pattern fits the white keys. Other starting notes need black keys. More on that next."
        }
      ],
      "alternative": {
        "text": "Picture a walk up a hill. Most steps are big strides: [[tone|tones]]. Two are small shuffles: [[semitone|semitones]]. Stride, stride, shuffle. Stride, stride, stride, shuffle. That last shuffle is what pulls you up onto the top note, and it is why the scale sounds finished.",
        "exhibit": {
          "kind": "keyboard",
          "highlight": [
            "E4",
            "F4",
            "B4",
            "C5"
          ],
          "low": "C4",
          "high": "C5"
        }
      }
    },
